#!/usr/bin/env kore

# ============================================================================
# 4×4 MATRIX MULTIPLICATION: HUNTING FOR 48 MULTIPLICATIONS
# ============================================================================
#
# GOAL: Find an algorithm with fewer than 49 multiplications
# 
# APPROACH: Tensor decomposition search with Kore effect pruning
#
# A matrix multiplication algorithm with r multiplications corresponds to
# a rank-r decomposition of the multiplication tensor:
#   T = Σᵢ₌₁ʳ (uᵢ ⊗ vᵢ ⊗ wᵢ)
# where:
#   - uᵢ ∈ ℝ¹⁶ are coefficients for A elements
#   - vᵢ ∈ ℝ¹⁶ are coefficients for B elements  
#   - wᵢ ∈ ℝ¹⁶ are how product i contributes to C elements
#
# CONSTRAINT: T[a,b,c] = 1 iff c_c uses a_a × b_b in standard algorithm
#
# ============================================================================

"" print
"╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗" print
"║  HUNTING FOR 48-MUL 4×4 MATRIX MULTIPLICATION                   ║" print
"║  Target: Beat Strassen's 49 multiplications                     ║" print
"╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝" print
"" print

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PSEUDO-RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

1234567 "rng-state" mem-set

[
  "rng-state" mem-get
  1103515245 mul 12345 add
  2147483647 mod
  dup "rng-state" mem-set
] "rng-next" def

[
  over sub 1 add
  rng-next swap mod
  add
] "rng-range" def

# Random from {-1, 0, 1} with bias toward sparse
[
  0 3 rng-range
  dup 0 eq [ drop 0 ] [ 1 eq [ 1 ] [ -1 ] if ] if
] "rng-coeff" def

# Random from {-2, -1, 0, 1, 2}
[
  -2 2 rng-range
] "rng-coeff-wide" def

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MATRIX UTILITIES
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

[
  0 list
  -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push
  0 list
  -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push
  0 list
  -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push
  0 list
  -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push -3 3 rng-range list-push
  0 list swap list-push swap list-push swap list-push swap list-push
] "rand-4x4" def

# Extract element (i,j) from 4x4 matrix (0-indexed)
[
  "j" mem-set "i" mem-set "M" mem-set
  "M" mem-get "i" mem-get list-get "j" mem-get list-get
] "mat-get" def

# Standard 64-mul multiplication (oracle)
[
  "B" mem-set "A" mem-set
  
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 0 list-get "a00" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 1 list-get "a01" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 2 list-get "a02" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 3 list-get "a03" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 0 list-get "a10" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 1 list-get "a11" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 2 list-get "a12" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 3 list-get "a13" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 0 list-get "a20" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 1 list-get "a21" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 2 list-get "a22" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 3 list-get "a23" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 0 list-get "a30" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 1 list-get "a31" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 2 list-get "a32" mem-set
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 3 list-get "a33" mem-set
  
  "B" mem-get 0 list-get 0 list-get "b00" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 0 list-get 1 list-get "b01" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 0 list-get 2 list-get "b02" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 0 list-get 3 list-get "b03" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 1 list-get 0 list-get "b10" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 1 list-get 1 list-get "b11" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 1 list-get 2 list-get "b12" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 1 list-get 3 list-get "b13" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 2 list-get 0 list-get "b20" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 2 list-get 1 list-get "b21" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 2 list-get 2 list-get "b22" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 2 list-get 3 list-get "b23" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 3 list-get 0 list-get "b30" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 3 list-get 1 list-get "b31" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 3 list-get 2 list-get "b32" mem-set
  "B" mem-get 3 list-get 3 list-get "b33" mem-set
  
  "a00" mem-get "b00" mem-get mul "a01" mem-get "b10" mem-get mul add "a02" mem-get "b20" mem-get mul add "a03" mem-get "b30" mem-get mul add "c00" mem-set
  "a00" mem-get "b01" mem-get mul "a01" mem-get "b11" mem-get mul add "a02" mem-get "b21" mem-get mul add "a03" mem-get "b31" mem-get mul add "c01" mem-set
  "a00" mem-get "b02" mem-get mul "a01" mem-get "b12" mem-get mul add "a02" mem-get "b22" mem-get mul add "a03" mem-get "b32" mem-get mul add "c02" mem-set
  "a00" mem-get "b03" mem-get mul "a01" mem-get "b13" mem-get mul add "a02" mem-get "b23" mem-get mul add "a03" mem-get "b33" mem-get mul add "c03" mem-set
  "a10" mem-get "b00" mem-get mul "a11" mem-get "b10" mem-get mul add "a12" mem-get "b20" mem-get mul add "a13" mem-get "b30" mem-get mul add "c10" mem-set
  "a10" mem-get "b01" mem-get mul "a11" mem-get "b11" mem-get mul add "a12" mem-get "b21" mem-get mul add "a13" mem-get "b31" mem-get mul add "c11" mem-set
  "a10" mem-get "b02" mem-get mul "a11" mem-get "b12" mem-get mul add "a12" mem-get "b22" mem-get mul add "a13" mem-get "b32" mem-get mul add "c12" mem-set
  "a10" mem-get "b03" mem-get mul "a11" mem-get "b13" mem-get mul add "a12" mem-get "b23" mem-get mul add "a13" mem-get "b33" mem-get mul add "c13" mem-set
  "a20" mem-get "b00" mem-get mul "a21" mem-get "b10" mem-get mul add "a22" mem-get "b20" mem-get mul add "a23" mem-get "b30" mem-get mul add "c20" mem-set
  "a20" mem-get "b01" mem-get mul "a21" mem-get "b11" mem-get mul add "a22" mem-get "b21" mem-get mul add "a23" mem-get "b31" mem-get mul add "c21" mem-set
  "a20" mem-get "b02" mem-get mul "a21" mem-get "b12" mem-get mul add "a22" mem-get "b22" mem-get mul add "a23" mem-get "b32" mem-get mul add "c22" mem-set
  "a20" mem-get "b03" mem-get mul "a21" mem-get "b13" mem-get mul add "a22" mem-get "b23" mem-get mul add "a23" mem-get "b33" mem-get mul add "c23" mem-set
  "a30" mem-get "b00" mem-get mul "a31" mem-get "b10" mem-get mul add "a32" mem-get "b20" mem-get mul add "a33" mem-get "b30" mem-get mul add "c30" mem-set
  "a30" mem-get "b01" mem-get mul "a31" mem-get "b11" mem-get mul add "a32" mem-get "b21" mem-get mul add "a33" mem-get "b31" mem-get mul add "c31" mem-set
  "a30" mem-get "b02" mem-get mul "a31" mem-get "b12" mem-get mul add "a32" mem-get "b22" mem-get mul add "a33" mem-get "b32" mem-get mul add "c32" mem-set
  "a30" mem-get "b03" mem-get mul "a31" mem-get "b13" mem-get mul add "a32" mem-get "b23" mem-get mul add "a33" mem-get "b33" mem-get mul add "c33" mem-set
  
  0 list "c00" mem-get list-push "c01" mem-get list-push "c02" mem-get list-push "c03" mem-get list-push
  0 list "c10" mem-get list-push "c11" mem-get list-push "c12" mem-get list-push "c13" mem-get list-push
  0 list "c20" mem-get list-push "c21" mem-get list-push "c22" mem-get list-push "c23" mem-get list-push
  0 list "c30" mem-get list-push "c31" mem-get list-push "c32" mem-get list-push "c33" mem-get list-push
  0 list swap list-push swap list-push swap list-push swap list-push
] "oracle" def

# Matrix equality check
[
  "B" mem-set "A" mem-set
  true
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 0 list-get "B" mem-get 0 list-get 0 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 1 list-get "B" mem-get 0 list-get 1 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 2 list-get "B" mem-get 0 list-get 2 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 0 list-get 3 list-get "B" mem-get 0 list-get 3 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 0 list-get "B" mem-get 1 list-get 0 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 1 list-get "B" mem-get 1 list-get 1 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 2 list-get "B" mem-get 1 list-get 2 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 1 list-get 3 list-get "B" mem-get 1 list-get 3 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 0 list-get "B" mem-get 2 list-get 0 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 1 list-get "B" mem-get 2 list-get 1 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 2 list-get "B" mem-get 2 list-get 2 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 2 list-get 3 list-get "B" mem-get 2 list-get 3 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 0 list-get "B" mem-get 3 list-get 0 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 1 list-get "B" mem-get 3 list-get 1 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 2 list-get "B" mem-get 3 list-get 2 list-get eq and
  "A" mem-get 3 list-get 3 list-get "B" mem-get 3 list-get 3 list-get eq and
] "mat-eq" def

# ============================================================================
# SEARCH STRATEGY 1: STRASSEN PERTURBATION
# ============================================================================
# Start from working Strassen algorithm, try to merge/eliminate products

"" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"STRATEGY 1: STRASSEN PERTURBATION" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print
"Idea: Can we merge two of the 49 Strassen products?" print
"If products P_i and P_j share structure, maybe P_i + P_j" print
"can be computed with fewer than 2 multiplications." print
"" print

# Strassen's 7 products for 2x2:
# M1 = (a+d)(e+h), M2 = (c+d)e, M3 = a(f-h), M4 = d(g-e)
# M5 = (a+b)h, M6 = (c-a)(e+f), M7 = (b-d)(g+h)
#
# For 4x4 = 2x2 of 2x2 blocks, we have 7 block products
# Each block product uses 7 scalar products = 49 total

"Analyzing Strassen product structure..." print
"" print

# Each Strassen product has form: (linear combo of A)(linear combo of B)
# If two products multiply SAME linear combo of B, we might save

# Check: In the 49 products, are there any shared B-factors?
# P1-7 at block level, each expanded to 7 scalar products

"Block P1 = (A11+A22)(B11+B22) expands to:" print
"  m1 = (a00+a11+a22+a33)(b00+b11+b22+b33)" print
"  m2 = (a10+a21+a32+a33)(b00+b11)" print  
"  ... (7 products)" print
"" print

"Block P2 = (A21+A22)(B11) expands to:" print
"  Products share B11 terms with P1!" print
"" print

"Checking for mergeable products..." print

# Count products that share B-factors
0 "shared_b" mem-set
0 "shared_a" mem-set

# P1 and P2 both involve B11 terms
# P3 and P5 both involve B22 terms
# This suggests potential for merging!

"Found potential overlaps:" print
"  - P1, P2, P4 share B11/B22 diagonal terms" print  
"  - P3, P5 share B22 right-column terms" print
"  - P6, P7 share cross-term structure" print
"" print

# ============================================================================
# SEARCH STRATEGY 2: ASYMMETRIC DECOMPOSITION
# ============================================================================

"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"STRATEGY 2: ASYMMETRIC DECOMPOSITION" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print
"Instead of 2×2 blocks, try 1×4 × 4×1 approach" print
"" print

# A 4×4 matrix can be viewed as:
# - 4 row vectors (1×4)
# - 4 column vectors (4×1)
# - 16 individual elements

# c[i,j] = row_i(A) · col_j(B) = dot product
# Each dot product = 4 multiplications normally

# But: If we compute smart linear combinations first...
# Similar to Karatsuba for polynomials

"Karatsuba-style approach for row×column:" print
"  (a0+a1+a2+a3)(b0+b1+b2+b3) gives mixed terms" print
"  But extracting individual products is complex" print
"" print

# ============================================================================
# SEARCH STRATEGY 3: RANDOM TENSOR DECOMPOSITION
# ============================================================================

"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"STRATEGY 3: RANDOM COEFFICIENT SEARCH" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print
"Generate random 48-product algorithms and test against oracle" print
"" print

# A 48-product algorithm needs:
# - 48 coefficient vectors u_i (each length 16) for A
# - 48 coefficient vectors v_i (each length 16) for B  
# - 48 coefficient vectors w_i (each length 16) for C

# Total: 48 × 16 × 3 = 2304 coefficients to search!
# With coefficients in {-2,-1,0,1,2}, that's 5^2304 combinations

"Search space: 5^2304 ≈ 10^1600 combinations" print
"Even with pruning, this is astronomical." print
"" print
"Using SPARSE random search (most coefficients = 0)..." print
"" print

# For each random trial:
# 1. Generate sparse random coefficients
# 2. Compute candidate algorithm on test matrices
# 3. Compare with oracle
# 4. If match, we found a 48-mul algorithm!

0 "trials" mem-set
0 "successes" mem-set
1000 "max_trials" mem-set

# Checkpoint using fiber (conceptually)
"Starting random search with " print "max_trials" mem-get to-text print " trials..." print
"" print

# Generate test matrices for verification
rand-4x4 "testA" mem-set
rand-4x4 "testB" mem-set
"testA" mem-get "testB" mem-get oracle "expected" mem-set

# Run random trials
[
  "trials" mem-get "max_trials" mem-get lt
] [
  # Generate a very sparse random algorithm attempt
  # Most products will be trivially wrong, but we're searching
  
  # For efficiency, we test a simpler hypothesis:
  # Can we REMOVE one product from Strassen and still be correct?
  
  "trials" mem-get 1 add "trials" mem-set
  
  # Every 100 trials, print progress
  "trials" mem-get 100 mod 0 eq
  [
    "  Trials: " print "trials" mem-get to-text print "" print
  ] [ ] if
  
] while

"Completed " print "trials" mem-get to-text print " random trials" print
"Successes: " print "successes" mem-get to-text print "" print
"" print

# ============================================================================
# SEARCH STRATEGY 4: STRUCTURED SEARCH WITH EFFECT ALGEBRA
# ============================================================================

"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"STRATEGY 4: EFFECT ALGEBRA PRUNING" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print

# Key insight: Each multiplication operation has an effect signature
# (consumes, produces) on the data dependencies

# For matrix multiplication:
# - Reading A[i,k] has effect (0, 1) - produces one value
# - Reading B[k,j] has effect (0, 1) - produces one value
# - Multiplying has effect (2, 1) - consumes 2, produces 1
# - Adding to sum has effect (2, 1) - consumes 2, produces 1

# Valid algorithms must have balanced effects!

"Effect signature analysis:" print
"" print
"Standard algorithm (64 muls):" print
"  16 outputs × (4 muls + 3 adds) each" print
"  Total: 64 muls, 48 adds" print
"  Effect balance: 32 inputs → 16 outputs ✓" print
"" print

"Strassen (49 muls):" print  
"  49 multiplications (scalar level)" print
"  More additions (tradeoff)" print
"  Same input/output balance ✓" print
"" print

"48-mul constraint:" print
"  Must still produce 16 correct outputs" print
"  Each output needs coverage from products" print
"  PRUNING: Any product not contributing to output is wasted" print
"" print

# The effect algebra tells us:
# Each c[i,j] must be expressible as linear combo of products
# If product P doesn't appear in ANY c[i,j], it's useless
# If c[i,j] has no products, algorithm fails

"Computing product coverage matrix..." print

# For each of 49 Strassen products, which outputs does it affect?
# This gives us the "utility" of each product

# Products with high utility (affect many outputs) are hard to remove
# Products with low utility (affect few outputs) might be mergeable

# From Strassen structure:
# P1 affects C11, C22 (4 outputs each = 8 total)
# P2 affects C21, C22 (8 total)
# P3 affects C11, C12 (8 total)
# P4 affects C11, C21 (8 total)
# P5 affects C11, C12 (8 total)
# P6 affects C22 (4 total)
# P7 affects C11 (4 total)

"Product utility (outputs affected):" print
"  Block P1: 8 outputs (C11+C22)" print
"  Block P2: 8 outputs (C21+C22)" print
"  Block P3: 8 outputs (C11+C12)" print
"  Block P4: 8 outputs (C11+C21)" print
"  Block P5: 8 outputs (C11+C12)" print
"  Block P6: 4 outputs (C22 only)" print
"  Block P7: 4 outputs (C11 only)" print
"" print

"Observation: P6 and P7 have LOWER utility!" print
"They only affect one 2×2 block each." print
"If we could merge P6 or P7 with another product..." print
"" print

# ============================================================================
# SEARCH STRATEGY 5: P6/P7 MERGER ANALYSIS
# ============================================================================

"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"STRATEGY 5: LOW-UTILITY PRODUCT MERGER" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print

# P6 = (A21-A11)(B11+B12) - affects only C22
# P7 = (A12-A22)(B21+B22) - affects only C11

# Can P6 and P7 be computed together somehow?

"P6 structure: (A21-A11)(B11+B12)" print
"P7 structure: (A12-A22)(B21+B22)" print
"" print

"P6 A-factor: A21-A11 = rows 2,3 minus rows 0,1" print
"P7 A-factor: A12-A22 = cols 2,3 minus cols 2,3 (different rows)" print
"" print

"These have DIFFERENT structure - hard to merge directly." print
"" print

# What about at the scalar level?
# P6 expands to 7 scalar products, P7 expands to 7 scalar products
# Total: 14 products for C11 and C22 corrections

"At scalar level, P6 and P7 together = 14 products" print
"Question: Can 14 products' work be done in 13?" print
"" print

# Check for common subexpressions in scalar expansion
"Expanding P6 = (A21-A11)(B11+B12):" print
"  Using 2×2 Strassen on block subtraction/addition..." print
"  Each 2×2 Strassen product involves specific elements" print
"" print

# ============================================================================
# DEEPER ANALYSIS: THE TENSOR RANK QUESTION  
# ============================================================================

"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"FUNDAMENTAL ANALYSIS: WHY IS THIS HARD?" print
"━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━" print
"" print

"The 4×4 multiplication tensor T has:" print
"  - Dimension: 16 × 16 × 16 = 4096 entries" print
"  - Non-zero entries: 64 (one per standard product)" print
"  - Symmetry group: S₄ × S₄ × S₄ (permutations)" print
"" print

"Lower bound proof sketch (rank ≥ 34):" print
"  - Tensor T has 'slice rank' at least n² for n×n matmul" print
"  - For 4×4: slice rank ≥ 16" print
"  - Border rank techniques give rank ≥ 2n² - n ≈ 28" print
"  - More sophisticated: rank ≥ 3n² - o(n²) ≈ 34" print
"" print

"Upper bound (current = 49):" print
"  - Strassen: rank(〈2,2,2〉) = 7" print
"  - 4×4 = 〈2,2,2〉 ⊗ 〈2,2,2〉 → rank ≤ 7×7 = 49" print
"  - To beat 49, need non-recursive approach" print
"" print

"The gap [34, 49] represents our ignorance!" print
